          228.0954,
          36.946894,
          386.261633,
          45.853459
        ],
        "raw_text": "CHAPTER 0. POLYNOMIAL RINGS",
        "kind": "running-head"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b003",
        "bbox": [
          53.2614,
          64.355659,
          119.411728,
          77.252892
        ],
        "raw_text": "Section 0.1",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b004",
        "bbox": [
          134.417853,
          64.355659,
          209.061132,
          77.252892
        ],
        "raw_text": "Polynomials",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b005",
        "bbox": [
          53.261395,
          85.670893,
          243.101241,
          142.397458
        ],
        "raw_text": "Polynomials and their solutions are some of the first objects we encounter in our mathematical lives. For example, you may even remember the first time you learned that the solutions in R 2 of the two-variable polynomial equation",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b006",
        "bbox": [
          114.725395,
          149.490842,
          181.930694,
          161.827989
        ],
        "raw_text": "x 2 + y 2 − 1 = 0",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b007",
        "bbox": [
          53.26139,
          171.036893,
          386.261287,
          203.853458
        ],
        "raw_text": "describe the unit circle. However, if our goal is to study the unit circle, then there are many other polynomials that one might choose to describe it; for example, the unit circle is also equal to the solutions of either",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b008",
        "bbox": [
          124.67739,
          210.945842,
          202.139689,
          223.283989
        ],
        "raw_text": "2x 2 + 2y 2 − 2 = 0",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b009",
        "bbox": [
          212.932389,
          213.719893,
          221.231235,
          222.626458
        ],
        "raw_text": "or",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b010",
        "bbox": [
          232.148389,
          210.945842,
          314.845338,
          223.283989
        ],
        "raw_text": "( x 2 + y 2 − 1 ) 2 = 0.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b011",
        "bbox": [
          53.26138,
          232.492893,
          386.262725,
          360.951458
        ],
        "raw_text": "This leads to a natural question: is there a best polynomial that describes the unit circle? The answer proposed by algebraic geometry is, in some sense, the most egalitarian: all of the polynomials that describe the unit circle are equally important, and we should study them together as a set. What does it mean, then, to study a set of polynomials? As we will soon learn, the set of polynomials describing the unit circle is much more than just a set; it has important algebraic structure that reflects the geometry of the circle. To be able to describe this algebraic structure in this example and beyond, we must first establish precise notation and terminology regarding sets of polynomi- als and their algebraic structure. To begin our formal discussion of polynomials, we start with the notion of a monomial.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b012",
        "bbox": [
          67.9344,
          377.582876,
          80.38765,
          386.48944
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.1",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b013",
        "bbox": [
          88.1594,
          376.088431,
          206.636098,
          386.836156
        ],
        "raw_text": "D EFINITION Monomials",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b014",
        "bbox": [
          67.934397,
          394.100521,
          337.859431,
          405.285709
        ],
        "raw_text": "A monomial in the variables x 1 , . . . , x n is an expression of the form",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b015",
        "bbox": [
          214.202394,
          414.531007,
          218.200198,
          420.27028
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b016",
        "bbox": [
          184.864394,
          415.424693,
          254.952043,
          428.797709
        ],
        "raw_text": "x α = x 1 1 · · · x n α n ,",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b017",
        "bbox": [
          67.934401,
          435.664048,
          371.589112,
          459.235439
        ],
        "raw_text": "where α = ( α 1 , . . . , α n ) ∈ N n is the exponent vector. Two monomials are equal if and only if they have the same exponent vector.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b018",
        "bbox": [
          53.261396,
          475.36653,
          386.261298,
          569.05498
        ],
        "raw_text": "The variables x 1 , . . . , x n should be When there are only a few variables, viewed as formal symbols, and their they are often represented with dis- role is simply to serve as placeholders tinct letters such as x, y, and z. for α 1 , . . . , α n . The data of a monomial in x 1 , . . . , x n is equivalent to the data of an exponent vector ( α 1 , . . . , α n ) ; however, placing each α i as the exponent of x i will prove useful when multiplication of monomials is defined below. Variables that appear with an exponent of 0 are typically omitted; for example,",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b019",
        "bbox": [
          151.8354,
          575.490833,
          287.981349,
          587.82798
        ],
        "raw_text": "x 2 y 3 z 0 = x 2 y 3 and x 0 y 0 z 0 = 1.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p015-b020",
        "bbox": [
          53.261399,
          594.760833,
          386.261296,
          630.301765
        ],
        "raw_text": "As in the case of f ( x, y ) = x 2 + y 2 − 1, polynomials are built by taking linear combinations of monomials. In the most general setting, the coefficients of these linear combinations belong to an arbitrary ring R, as in the following definition.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "pdf_page": 16,
    "printed_page": 3,
    "page_size_bp": [
      439.425,
      666.224
    ],
    "side_note_starts": [
      "p016-b014"
    ],
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p016-b001",
        "bbox": [
          52.455002,
          36.946603,
          143.376621,
          45.854035
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.1. POLYNOMIALS",
        "kind": "running-head"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b002",
        "bbox": [
          380.474105,
          36.946603,
          385.455605,
          45.853525
        ],
        "raw_text": "3",
        "kind": "running-head"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b003",
        "bbox": [
          67.127998,
          70.497023,
          239.568423,
          81.345866
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.2 D EFINITION Polynomials over R",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b004",
        "bbox": [
          67.128103,
          88.507237,
          370.788675,
          99.693827
        ],
        "raw_text": "A polynomial in the variables x 1 , . . . , x n over R is an expression of the form",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b005",
        "bbox": [
          153.426103,
          110.414109,
          238.153034,
          121.611827
        ],
        "raw_text": "f = f ( x 1 , . . . , x n ) =",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b006",
        "bbox": [
          246.211102,
          106.439923,
          285.903852,
          127.67978
        ],
        "raw_text": "∑ n a α x α ,",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b007",
        "bbox": [
          241.156102,
          121.114122,
          257.900646,
          130.957722
        ],
        "raw_text": "α ∈ N",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b008",
        "bbox": [
          67.128098,
          138.385672,
          370.782131,
          178.714322
        ],
        "raw_text": "where a α ∈ R for each α ∈ N n and a α = 0 for all but finitely many α. Two polynomials f = ∑ a α x α and g = ∑ b α x α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b009",
        "bbox": [
          175.071106,
          177.874818,
          179.069122,
          183.614394
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b010",
        "bbox": [
          67.128103,
          189.639109,
          192.254119,
          200.427394
        ],
        "raw_text": "are equal if and only if a α = b α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b011",
        "bbox": [
          257.111105,
          177.874818,
          261.109121,
          183.614394
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b012",
        "bbox": [
          195.337103,
          187.378672,
          257.225852,
          200.330572
        ],
        "raw_text": "for all α ∈ N n .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b013",
        "bbox": [
          54.946106,
          225.90654,
          187.223068,
          236.654085
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.3 E XAMPLE Polynomials",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b014",
        "bbox": [
          231.770996,
          235.489243,
          377.938236,
          267.987349
        ],
        "raw_text": "Polynomials are typically written as finite sums, omitting all summands that have a coefficient of zero.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b015",
        "bbox": [
          52.455097,
          244.356582,
          209.003693,
          278.508546
        ],
        "raw_text": "The following are two examples of polynomials in the variables x and y over the ring of integers Z:",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b016",
        "bbox": [
          136.070099,
          283.675515,
          303.260348,
          296.013061
        ],
        "raw_text": "f = xy 2 + 3xy + 2 and g = − xy + 4.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b017",
        "bbox": [
          52.455105,
          301.920644,
          385.455284,
          337.895837
        ],
        "raw_text": "The polynomials f and g can also be viewed as having coefficients in Q, R, C or any other ring containing Z. Observe that we can create new polynomials from f and g by adding them and multiplying them in the familiar way:",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b018",
        "bbox": [
          170.506138,
          345.031515,
          268.824388,
          357.36906
        ],
        "raw_text": "f + g = xy 2 + 2xy + 6,",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b019",
        "bbox": [
          133.481135,
          364.294515,
          305.849383,
          376.63306
        ],
        "raw_text": "f g = − x 2 y 3 − 3x 2 y 2 + 4xy 2 + 10xy + 8.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b020",
        "bbox": [
          52.455108,
          391.670572,
          385.468423,
          424.487494
        ],
        "raw_text": "As the reader is encouraged to verify in Exercise 0.1.1, the operations of addition and multiplication, formalized in the next definition, endow the set of polynomials with the structure of a ring.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b021",
        "bbox": [
          67.127998,
          441.525053,
          229.11509,
          452.272598
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.4 D EFINITION Polynomial rings",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b022",
        "bbox": [
          67.128096,
          459.524079,
          370.79161,
          510.596294
        ],
        "raw_text": "The polynomial ring R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] is the set of all polynomials in variables x 1 , . . . , x n over R. Polynomial addition and multiplication are defined by [extractor U+0010] [extractor U+0011] [extractor U+0010] [extractor U+0011] ∑ a α x α + ∑ b α x α = ∑ ( a α + b α ) x α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b023",
        "bbox": [
          140.575092,
          509.755788,
          144.573108,
          515.495364
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b024",
        "bbox": [
          67.128089,
          525.47258,
          81.514661,
          534.379502
        ],
        "raw_text": "and",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b025",
        "bbox": [
          118.815089,
          533.717974,
          124.763,
          540.094294
        ],
        "raw_text": "[extractor U+0010]",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b026",
        "bbox": [
          197.462091,
          509.755789,
          201.460107,
          515.495365
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b027",
        "bbox": [
          157.313089,
          533.717975,
          169.458026,
          540.094295
        ],
        "raw_text": "[extractor U+0011][extractor U+0010]",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b028",
        "bbox": [
          249.704091,
          509.75579,
          253.702107,
          515.495366
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b029",
        "bbox": [
          201.898088,
          533.717975,
          207.845999,
          540.094295
        ],
        "raw_text": "[extractor U+0011]",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b030",
        "bbox": [
          125.012089,
          534.001894,
          232.646204,
          552.134295
        ],
        "raw_text": "∑ a α x α ∑ b α x α = ∑",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b031",
        "bbox": [
          128.363089,
          551.29379,
          132.361105,
          557.033366
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b032",
        "bbox": [
          173.057088,
          551.293791,
          177.055104,
          557.033367
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b033",
        "bbox": [
          225.299088,
          551.293791,
          229.297104,
          557.033367
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b034",
        "bbox": [
          232.897088,
          533.717976,
          238.844999,
          540.094296
        ],
        "raw_text": "[extractor U+0010]",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b035",
        "bbox": [
          251.369088,
          534.001895,
          262.067204,
          552.134295
        ],
        "raw_text": "∑",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b036",
        "bbox": [
          240.724088,
          549.749836,
          272.714104,
          558.479291
        ],
        "raw_text": "α 1 + α 2 = α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b037",
        "bbox": [
          274.743088,
          533.717975,
          319.219838,
          549.928291
        ],
        "raw_text": "[extractor U+0011] a α 1 b α 2 x α .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p016-b038",
        "bbox": [
          52.455099,
          573.240749,
          385.45596,
          631.779802
        ],
        "raw_text": "The additive identity 0 ∈ R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] is the polynomial for which a α = 0 for all α, and the multiplicative identity 1 ∈ R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] is the polynomial for which ( 1 if ( α 1 , . . . , α n ) = ( 0, . . . , 0 ) , a α = 0 if ( α 1 , . . . , α n ) ̸ = ( 0, . . . , 0 ) .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "pdf_page": 17,
    "printed_page": 4,
    "page_size_bp": [
      439.425,
      666.224
    ],
    "side_note_starts": [
      "p017-b016"
    ],
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p017-b001",
        "bbox": [
          228.095001,
          36.946603,
          386.265849,
          45.854035
        ],
        "raw_text": "CHAPTER 0. POLYNOMIAL RINGS",
        "kind": "running-head"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b002",
        "bbox": [
          53.261002,
          36.947113,
          58.242502,
          45.854035
        ],
        "raw_text": "4",
        "kind": "running-head"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b003",
        "bbox": [
          53.261385,
          64.909602,
          386.274649,
          97.726524
        ],
        "raw_text": "When working with polynomial rings, it can be useful to leverage their recursive nature in order to utilize proofs by induction on the number of variables. The next result is somewhat self-evident, but we state it carefully as it will be used often.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b004",
        "bbox": [
          67.933998,
          116.562025,
          319.337337,
          127.30957
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.5 P ROPOSITION Recursive nature of polynomial rings",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b005",
        "bbox": [
          67.934404,
          135.011611,
          235.621657,
          143.918533
        ],
        "raw_text": "There is a canonical isomorphism of rings",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b006",
        "bbox": [
          143.749407,
          156.479111,
          296.097155,
          167.676829
        ],
        "raw_text": "R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] = R [ x 1 , . . . , x n − 1 ][ x n ] ,",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b007",
        "bbox": [
          67.9344,
          178.408238,
          371.588617,
          201.549828
        ],
        "raw_text": "where the right-hand side is the ring of polynomials in one variable x n with coefficients in the ring R [ x 1 , . . . , x n − 1 ] .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b008",
        "bbox": [
          68.205399,
          218.400583,
          332.174902,
          227.307532
        ],
        "raw_text": "The next example illustrates the main idea behind Proposition 0.5.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b009",
        "bbox": [
          55.751999,
          240.126651,
          229.23994,
          253.078551
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.6 E XAMPLE Z [ x, y ] versus Z [ x ][ y ]",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b010",
        "bbox": [
          53.261407,
          259.791587,
          151.785514,
          268.698509
        ],
        "raw_text": "Consider the polynomial",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b011",
        "bbox": [
          134.500405,
          279.986521,
          306.442154,
          293.002551
        ],
        "raw_text": "f = x 3 y 2 + xy 2 − 2xy − x + 1 ∈ Z [ x, y ] .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b012",
        "bbox": [
          53.261393,
          303.018651,
          386.264882,
          327.039844
        ],
        "raw_text": "We can view f as an element of Z [ x ][ y ] by grouping all terms that have the same exponent in y. In doing so, we write",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b013",
        "bbox": [
          117.064392,
          338.960521,
          323.87814,
          351.97655
        ],
        "raw_text": "f = ( x 3 + x ) y 2 + (− 2x ) y + (− x + 1 ) ∈ Z [ x ][ y ] .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b014",
        "bbox": [
          53.261383,
          362.42752,
          386.2667,
          386.90055
        ],
        "raw_text": "As a polynomial in y, the coefficients of f are x 3 + x, − 2x, and − x + 1, all of which are elements of Z [ x ] .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b015",
        "bbox": [
          53.261396,
          401.093591,
          209.810088,
          506.167501
        ],
        "raw_text": "P ROOF OF P ROPOSITION 0.5 To prove the proposition, we describe the canonical ring isomorphism, which, as illustrated in Example 0.6, sim- ply groups all terms of a polynomial in R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] for which x n appears with the same exponent. To make this more precise, consider the following function:",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b016",
        "bbox": [
          232.576996,
          406.662247,
          378.744607,
          498.936353
        ],
        "raw_text": "When two rings are isomorphic, there will often be a multitude of possible isomorphisms. The word “canonical” means that there is one natural choice among all possible isomorphisms. The symbol ∼ = is used to denote isomorphisms, while = is used for canonical isomorphisms.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b017",
        "bbox": [
          114.613399,
          519.198743,
          330.059149,
          556.470293
        ],
        "raw_text": "φ : R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] → R [ x 1 , . . . , x n − 1 ][ x n ] [extractor U+0012] [extractor U+0013] α n − 1 α 1 α 1 α n ∑ a α x 1 · · · x n 7→ ∑ ∑ a α x 1 · · · x n − 1 x n d .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b018",
        "bbox": [
          109.558399,
          553.011092,
          129.890167,
          562.854692
        ],
        "raw_text": "α ∈ N n",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b019",
        "bbox": [
          201.992399,
          554.559993,
          215.981399,
          562.612797
        ],
        "raw_text": "d ≥ 0",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b020",
        "bbox": [
          228.153399,
          553.539377,
          244.921167,
          568.013751
        ],
        "raw_text": "α ∈ N n α n = d",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b021",
        "bbox": [
          53.261392,
          579.555577,
          302.815141,
          589.060279
        ],
        "raw_text": "The verification that φ is a ring isomorphism is Exercise 0.1.3.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p017-b022",
        "bbox": [
          53.261404,
          597.036584,
          386.273907,
          617.898506
        ],
        "raw_text": "The following result concerning polynomial rings over integral domains is a first application of Proposition 0.5.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "pdf_page": 18,
    "printed_page": 5,
    "page_size_bp": [
      439.425,
      666.224
    ],
    "side_note_starts": [],
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p018-b001",
        "bbox": [
          52.455002,
          36.946603,
          143.376621,
          45.854035
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.1. POLYNOMIALS",
        "kind": "running-head"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b002",
        "bbox": [
          380.474105,
          36.946603,
          385.455605,
          45.853525
        ],
        "raw_text": "5",
        "kind": "running-head"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b003",
        "bbox": [
          67.127998,
          70.497023,
          309.534428,
          81.244568
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.7 P ROPOSITION Polynomials over integral domains",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b004",
        "bbox": [
          67.128104,
          88.49611,
          337.67401,
          99.693827
        ],
        "raw_text": "If R is an integral domain, then R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] is an integral domain.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b005",
        "bbox": [
          55.471107,
          116.008609,
          373.617623,
          138.969833
        ],
        "raw_text": "P ROOF We proceed by induction on the number of variables. (Base case) Let f , g ∈ R [ x 1 ] be nonzero. We must prove that f g ̸ = 0. Write",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b006",
        "bbox": [
          66.164102,
          148.800029,
          200.900101,
          164.382833
        ],
        "raw_text": "f = a d x 1 d + a d − 1 x 1 d − 1 + · · · + a 0",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b007",
        "bbox": [
          212.191101,
          152.077615,
          226.577673,
          160.984537
        ],
        "raw_text": "and",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b008",
        "bbox": [
          237.7131,
          148.80003,
          373.165849,
          164.382834
        ],
        "raw_text": "g = b e x 1 e + b e − 1 x 1 e − 1 + · · · + b 0 ,",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b009",
        "bbox": [
          52.455098,
          173.47978,
          319.79785,
          185.326822
        ],
        "raw_text": "where a d ̸ = 0 and b e ̸ = 0. By definition of multiplication in R [ x 1 ] ,",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b010",
        "bbox": [
          97.259096,
          194.93503,
          342.070844,
          210.517834
        ],
        "raw_text": "f g = ( a d b e ) x 1 d + e + ( a d b e − 1 + a d − 1 b e ) x 1 d + e − 1 + · · · + a 0 b 0 .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b011",
        "bbox": [
          52.455087,
          219.614781,
          385.461719,
          267.516834
        ],
        "raw_text": "Since R is an integral domain, a d b e ̸ = 0. Since f g has at least one nonzero coeffi- cient, we conclude that f g ̸ = 0. (Induction step) Assume S = R [ x 1 , . . . , x n − 1 ] is an integral domain; we must show that R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] is an integral domain. By Proposition 0.5,",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b012",
        "bbox": [
          172.520088,
          278.752117,
          265.714838,
          289.949834
        ],
        "raw_text": "R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] = S [ x n ] .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b013",
        "bbox": [
          52.455091,
          301.196244,
          385.467267,
          324.337834
        ],
        "raw_text": "Since S is an integral domain, the argument used in the base case immediately im- plies that S [ x n ] , and thus R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] , is an integral domain.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b014",
        "bbox": [
          52.455103,
          342.320212,
          385.461727,
          375.575506
        ],
        "raw_text": "The numbers d and e appearing in the proof of Proposition 0.7 are important attributes of the polynomials f and g, called their degrees. The next definition gen- eralizes the notion of degree to polynomials with any number of variables.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b015",
        "bbox": [
          67.127998,
          393.876051,
          295.548384,
          404.623596
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.8 D EFINITION Monomial and polynomial degree",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b016",
        "bbox": [
          204.176095,
          410.398787,
          208.174111,
          416.138363
        ],
        "raw_text": "α",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b017",
        "bbox": [
          67.128096,
          410.809787,
          251.571415,
          424.666795
        ],
        "raw_text": "The degree of a monomial x α = x 1 1 · · · x n α n is",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b018",
        "bbox": [
          154.437096,
          431.53264,
          283.472844,
          444.990794
        ],
        "raw_text": "deg ( x α ) = α 1 + · · · + α n ∈ N.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b019",
        "bbox": [
          67.12809,
          454.217639,
          339.527409,
          467.169539
        ],
        "raw_text": "The degree of a nonzero polynomial f = ∑ a α x α ∈ R [ x 1 , . . . , x n ] is",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b020",
        "bbox": [
          147.433083,
          477.047739,
          290.477832,
          488.41736
        ],
        "raw_text": "deg ( f ) = max { deg ( x α ) | a α ̸ = 0 } .",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b021",
        "bbox": [
          54.945999,
          515.758055,
          166.01188,
          526.5056
        ],
        "raw_text": "0.9 E XAMPLE Degree",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b022",
        "bbox": [
          52.455105,
          531.930515,
          367.555249,
          543.771061
        ],
        "raw_text": "The monomials x 2 yz, z 4 , x, and 1 have degrees 4, 4, 1, and 0, respectively, and",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b023",
        "bbox": [
          159.049104,
          555.061515,
          278.861352,
          567.399061
        ],
        "raw_text": "deg ( x 2 yz + z 4 + x + 1 ) = 4.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      },
      {
        "id": "p018-b024",
        "bbox": [
          52.4551,
          589.436586,
          385.468385,
          623.58855
        ],
        "raw_text": "The reader may have noticed that the zero polynomial has not been assigned a degree, which is intentional. One of the most useful properties of degree is additivity, described in the next result, which fails for any choice of deg ( 0 ) ∈ N.",
        "kind": "source-layout-block"
      }
    ]
  }
]
